.TH FILEOPS 1 "October 2026" "fileops-cpp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fileops \- create, copy, combine and delete files safely
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fileops
[\fIGLOBAL OPTIONS\fR]
.I command
[\fIARGS\fR]
.br
.B fileops create
.I path
[\fB\-\-text\fR \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR]
.br
.B fileops copy
.I source destination
[\fB\-\-force\fR]
.br
.B fileops combine
.I first second output
[\fB\-\-force\fR]
.br
.B fileops delete
.I path
[\fB\-\-force\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fileops
performs four file operations with conservative defaults: an existing
destination is never overwritten unless
.B \-\-force
is given, and every write goes to a temporary file in the destination
directory that is renamed into place, so an interrupted run never leaves a
partially written destination (a run killed by a signal may leave a
.IB .name. id .tmp
sibling).
.PP
Nothing is written to standard output except by
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version .
Diagnostics and optional structured logs go to standard error. A usage error
prints one
.RI \(lq "fileops: message" \(rq
line per error and
.RB \(lq "fileops: run \(aqfileops \-\-help\(aq for usage" \(rq
on standard error, writes nothing to standard output and exits 1.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI create " path"
Create
.I path
as an empty file, or with the content given by
.B \-\-text
(UTF\-8, written exactly as given with no trailing newline).
.TP
.BI copy " source destination"
Copy
.I source
to
.IR destination ,
preserving Unix permission bits. Copying a file onto itself is an error.
.TP
.BI combine " first second output"
Write the bytes of
.I first
followed by the bytes of
.I second
to
.IR output .
Because inputs are fully read before the output is renamed into place,
.I output
may be one of the inputs when
.B \-\-force
is given, which appends in place.
.TP
.BI delete " path"
Delete
.IR path .
Directories are refused.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
For
.BR create ", " copy " and " combine :
overwrite an existing destination. For
.BR delete :
succeed even if the file does not exist (like
.BR "rm \-f" ).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-text " " \fITEXT\fR
Initial content for
.BR create .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Shorthand for
.BR "\-\-log\-level Information" .
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
Minimum log level written to standard error: Trace, Debug, Information,
Warning, Error, Critical or None (default None).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-format " FORMAT"
.B text
(default) or
.BR json ,
one JSON object per line with a stable schema (Timestamp, EventId, EventName,
LogLevel, Category, Message, State).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error: invalid arguments, empty path, source and destination are the
same file, or the path is a directory.
.TP
.B 2
A required input file or directory does not exist.
.TP
.B 3
The destination already exists and
.B \-\-force
was not given.
.TP
.B 4
The file system refused the operation (permission denied, disk full,
locked file).
.TP
.B 70
Internal error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FILEOPS_LOG_LEVEL
Default for
.BR \-\-log\-level .
.TP
.B FILEOPS_LOG_FORMAT
Default for
.BR \-\-log\-format .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
fileops create notes.txt \-\-text "hello"
fileops copy notes.txt backup.txt
fileops combine part1.bin part2.bin whole.bin
fileops combine log.txt today.txt log.txt \-\-force   # append in place
fileops delete backup.txt
FILEOPS_LOG_FORMAT=json fileops \-v copy a b 2>run.ndjson
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR rm (1)
.SH BUGS
https://github.com/andrewczwu/fileops-cpp/issues
